"A container image layer diagram shows ordered filesystem changes and distinguishes immutable image content from a running container’s writable state.\n\nUse when: Explain image reuse, build layering, and deletion behavior. Review what persists in an image versus a container instance.\n\nAvoid when: The main concern is network service placement.\n\nVisual grammar: Stacked layers encode ordered filesystem changes under the image format. Manifest or digest labels identify immutable image content. A distinct writable layer and external volumes represent runtime state.\n\nBuild: Name the image format and select a short representative build. Show base and added layers in their actual order. Separate runtime writes and mounted storage from the image stack. Place file-change callouts beside layers, keeping boundaries clear of labels.\n\nCheck: Deleting a file in a later layer is not described as removing earlier bytes. Shared layers and writable instance state remain distinct. Layer sizes are shown only with measurement and units. Stack borders and digest callouts do not cross file annotations.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a secret removed in a later layer was never included in the image. Treating a volume as another immutable image layer.\n\nExample: A base image gains dependency and application layers; a running container adds a separate writable layer and mounted data volume.\n\nAlternatives: Deployment diagram, Software supply-chain provenance graph."
